## **👋 About Me**

Hi, I’m Azani, a lecturer in Information Systems and a PhD candidate in Computer Science, with a passion for learning analytics, educational technology, and intelligent systems that empower learners.

Over the past few years, I have dedicated myself to exploring how data can enhance education. My current research focuses on developing a framework to transform low-level student activity logs into meaningful behavioral insights, helping educators better understand and support self-regulated learning in digital environments. I combine expertise in process mining, machine learning, and behavioral analytics to bridge the gap between raw educational data and impactful learning decisions.

As a lecturer, I love designing student-centered learning experiences using real-world case studies, mastery-based learning models, and active learning strategies. I’ve taught courses in Object-Oriented Programming, Databases, and Information Systems Strategy, and I always aim to make abstract concepts more approachable and applicable.

Beyond academia, I enjoy building personal productivity systems that integrate Islamic values with modern frameworks like OKR and the 12-Week Year. I also have hands-on experience with cloud computing (Azure, AWS), data visualization, and educational software development.

